From ff535ca5beac2ebfe84c9378e2fc7851b46ae879 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sergey Fedorov <barracuda@macos-powerpc.org>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 18:52:16 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 06/14] fix(vendor): CoreTextLocator for macOS 10.6: port
 upstream's 0.8.0 locator

Upstream 0.8.0 rewrote the macOS font locator (PR #2167): a configured
fallback chain resolved family by family with the family as a mandatory
match attribute, by-content fallback through CTFontCreateForString, and --
the part this port never had -- resolution of a CoreText face to the right
face INSIDE a .ttc collection (FontPath::collectionIndex), by matching the
face's PostScript name against the faces FreeType finds in the file, with
the file mapped once through HarfBuzz and the scan cached per file. Without
it a collection resolves to face 0 whatever CoreText picked, which is why
Menlo.ttc's bold and italic, and every .ttc CJK fallback, used to open the
regular face.

This takes that file and makes it build with GCC and run on 10.6:

- No Objective-C literals. GCC compiles neither `@{}` dictionaries nor
  boxed `@()` numbers nor `@autoreleasepool`, and `__bridge` casts are an
  ARC spelling. The descriptor's attributes and mandatory-attribute set are
  built with CFDictionaryCreate/CFSetCreate, numbers with CFNumberCreate,
  strings read with CFStringGetCString/CFURLGetFileSystemRepresentation.
  The one Objective-C call left (NSFontManager, below) runs under a small
  RAII NSAutoreleasePool.
- The NSFontWeight* constants (10.11+) are spelled as the numbers they
  stand for, in CoreText's -1..1 weight-trait scale.
- CTFontCopyDefaultCascadeListForLanguages (10.8+) is not used. Without an
  explicit fallback list the chain is the primary alone; a codepoint it
  lacks is resolved by content in resolve(), which asks CoreText directly
  which font renders it -- the face the cascade would eventually reach,
  found in one query rather than by walking a family-ordered list (the one
  CJK-capable face was observed 83rd of 201 on a real system, past any walk
  limit worth paying for). CoreTextLocator_test's "automatic cascade"
  expectation follows.
- kCTFontURLAttribute is frequently null for system fonts on 10.6: the
  path falls back to Apple Type Services (CTFontCopyPostScriptName ->
  ATSFontFindFromPostScriptName -> ATSFontGetFileReference -> FSRef), on
  32-bit builds, where those APIs exist; 64-bit builds have the URL.
- 10.6's CoreText, given a family plus a weight/slant traits dictionary,
  can answer with another family altogether (Lucida Grande) when the
  weight is not an exact match -- how bold text came out proportional.
  The resolved family is verified and, if wrong, the family's own bold or
  italic member is taken from NSFontManager fontWithFamily:traits:weight:
  size:, which gets this right.
- CoreText is included through the ApplicationServices umbrella; it is a
  standalone framework only from 10.8 on.
- The by-content anchor is Menlo, as upstream has it; Menlo has shipped
  with every release since 10.6.

Rebase note (v0.8.0.9335): replaces this port's own locator, which the
former portability, bold-spacing and by-content-fallback patches had built
up from upstream's pre-0.8.0 file (CFDictionary traits, the ATS path
fallback, the NSFontManager re-resolution, a resolve() on the same
CTFontCreateForString primitive anchored on Helvetica). Everything those
did for 10.6 is here; what upstream added on top is kept.

